    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d758">

                <pb facs="0652" n="640" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg640"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.24/272: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Burdett</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-12-03T12:00:00-03:00">December 3,
                        1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 12:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>626. For the Under Secretary. Reference Embassy’s telegram No. 625, December 2, 6
                        p.m.<anchor n="68" xml:id="d758-f1"/> The Minister for Foreign Affairs
                    states that he has heard nothing more about the <hi rendition="#italic">Siqueira
                        Campos</hi> and in view of this and the other two incidents concerning
                    Brazilian ships<anchor n="69" xml:id="d758-f2"/> he <pb facs="0653" n="641"
                        xml:id="pg641"/> asked me today to telegraph you that he is trying to
                    control public opinion and preserve good relations with Great Britain. In spite
                    of his best efforts he fears the natural reaction here and cannot guarantee what
                    will happen. He states that reprisals against British interests here may even
                    occur. He wants you to know that he is trying his best in the face of a very
                    delicate situation.</p>
                <p>The news is today released here that the <hi rendition="#italic">Buarque</hi> of
                    the Lloyd Brasileiro now at La Guaira was seized on November 26 by the British
                    and taken to Trinidad where 70 packages destined for La Guaira were confiscated
                    as contraband.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Burdett</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d758-f1" n="68" xml:id="d758-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d758-f2" n="69" xml:id="d758-f2-content">
                <p>The seizing by British naval vessels of two Brazilian merchant ships, the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Buarque and</hi> the <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Itapé</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
